{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>Westward and northward and southward and eastward.</b> Nowhere in the Torah do we find the directions mentioned in a single sequence, for sometimes Scripture puts the east first, as is written (Job 23:8–9): ״הן קדם אהלך ואיננו ואחור ולא אבין לו״ — 'Behold, I go forward but He is not there, and backward but I cannot perceive Him'; ״שמאול בעשותו ולא אחז יעטוף ימין ולא אראה״ — 'On the left where He works I cannot behold Him; He veils Himself on the right and I cannot see Him' — and this is the proper order, for they are set in a straight line from east to west and from north to south. Sometimes He puts west first, as is written to Jacob (Genesis 28:14): ״ופרצת ימה וקדמה וצפונה ונגבה״. And sometimes He puts north first, as He said to Abraham (ibid. 13:14): ״שא נא עיניך וראה מן המקום אשר אתה שם צפונה ונגבה וקדמה וימה״. And the reason He began with the westerly direction for both Jacob and Moses — it is possible to give a reason on account of the Shekhinah, which went down with Jacob to Egypt, and it is known that the Shekhinah is in the west; and here too Moses is speaking first to the Shekhinah, and he says תימנה and not נגבה, for half of the Name is alluded to in each and every direction — for thus did Moses praise the Holy One, blessed be He (Numbers 16:22): ״אל אלהי הרוחות לכל בשר״, 'El, Elohim of the spirits for all flesh.' And the reason he repeated עיניך twice and used the language of ascent is that from there he was elevated to see the Land with the physical eye in all its four directions, and to meditate there on the Name of four letters, which is the supreme first Chariot for the four directions of the world." "<b>And you must</b> know that the four directions of the world, which are east and west and north and south, each have three names: east — קדם, פנים; west — ים, אחור; and likewise south — נגב, ימין; and likewise north — אסתן, שמאל. The explanation: it is called the east (מזרח) on account of the sun's shining (זריחה) from there; קדם, because from that direction the sun begins (מקדים) to shine; and פנים, on account of the formation of man, whose face was created toward the east, his back toward the west, his right hand toward the south, his left hand toward the north. Similarly, it is called west (מערב) on account of the setting of the sun, when the forms intermingle (מתערבות) at that hour; ים, because the Great Sea is at the west of the world, and the Targum renders (Deuteronomy 34:2) ״הים האחרון״ as ״ימה מערבאה״; and אחור, corresponding to the east, which is called פנים — and this is what is written in Job (Job 23:8): ״הן קדם אהלך ואיננו ואחור ולא אבין לו״. Similarly, it is called south (דרום) because the sun travels at the height (גובה) of that direction, and it is a notarikon of דר רום or דרך רום, from the language of (Habakkuk 3:10) ״רום ידהו נשא״; and נגב, because that direction is parched (נגוב) and very dry, from the strength of the sun's heat that hovers over that direction — and this is what Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Ecclesiastes 1:6): ״הולך אל דרום וסובב אל צפון״ — he said of the south הולך and of the north סובב. And they have already mentioned in the books of inquiry that once a certain man of the south forgot a wooden bowl left in the sun at the summer solstice in Tammuz, and in the evening he found it melted and dissolved like wax melting in fire; therefore that direction is called נגב on account of the dryness and the heat — and this is what is written (Judges 1:15): ״כי ארץ הנגב נתתני״, and they translate ״ארע דרומא״; and ימין, on account of the right hand of a person who faces the east, or on account of the property (פעולה) that the sun is constantly present (מזומן) in that direction — from the language of (Daniel 1:5) ״וימן להם המלך״, (Psalms 61:8) ״חסד ואמת מן ינצרוהו״ — and from this, Israel's sustenance (מזון) in the wilderness is called in the language of מן, on account of the readiness (הזמנה) — sustenance that came to them prepared every day, in the manner of what is written (Exodus 16:4): ״ולקטו דבר יום ביומו״. Similarly, it is called north (צפון) on account of the sun being hidden (צפון) and concealed there, from the fact that that direction has no settlement, and our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: 'the north wind does not circle about,' as is written (Job 26:7): ״נוטה צפון על תהו״; and אסתן is the language of our Rabbis, of blessed memory: ״עדיה בצד אסתן ותאסר״, and the Targum renders (Genesis 8:22) ״קיץ וחרף״ as ״קיטא וסתוא״; and it is known that the rainy season (חרף) is the time of rains, for the north wind is very moist and the rains come from there — and for this reason Scripture says (Proverbs 25:23): ״רוח צפון תחולל גשם״ — for just as the south wind is hot and dry from the strength of the sun's heat that travels there constantly, so the north wind must be cold and moist, for the sun is hidden away from there. Also possible is that אסתן is from the language of אסותא (healing), for the north wind has healing in it, and thus our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: 'the north wind brings recovery' — and for this reason Israel did not circumcise in the wilderness, because the north wind did not blow for them; and the reason it did not blow — they gave two reasons for this in chapter הערל in Yevamot: if you wish, say because they were unwell (נזופין), and if you wish, say so as not to scatter the clouds of glory; and שמאל, on account of the left hand of a person who faces the east, or on account of its property that the sun is hidden from there, and therefore it is called שמאל from the language of סומא, from the language of a thing hidden (סמוי) from the eye — that is, because the sun concealed itself from there. And they said in the Midrash: 'The first man was formed from east to west; his face to the east, his back to the west,' as is written (Psalms 139:5): ״אחור וקדם צרתני״; 'the side that was toward the east — lights were fixed in it, and it was called פנים, corresponding to the face of the sun; the side that was toward the west was called אחור, corresponding to the back of the sun, which has no light; the side that was toward the south, where the sun constantly operates, was called ימין — just as a person's right hand operates in every task; the side that was toward the north, where the sun does not operate at all, was called שמאל — just as a person's left hand is idle from labor' — end of quotation." "<b>For you shall not cross this Jordan.</b> Not even your bones shall cross. What Moses mentions immediately afterward — ״כי אנכי מת בארץ הזאת״, 'For I am dying in this land,' ״אנכי לא עובר את הירדן״, 'I am not crossing the Jordan' — means that not even his bones cross. For if he did not know this, it would have been fitting for Moses to command Elazar and Joshua to bring his bones into the Land, or to adjure Israel as a whole, just as Joseph did. Now Jacob, who is the threefold cord among the patriarchs of the Chariot, merited in this that his body and his bones were buried in the Land; and Joseph merited not his body but only his bones; and Moses, the greatest of them all, merited neither his body nor his bones. And it was the will of the Holy One, blessed be He, that his bones not enter the Land, for the honor of those Israelites who died in the wilderness and whose bones did not enter the Land — and further, for the honor of all those who died in exile outside the Land and whose souls were bound to HaShem, kept for the morning of redemption — to make known that all of them will merit the revival of the dead through his merit, and they will rise with him."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:45:32.915782680Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}